const COURSE_12_QUIZZES_DEF = {
  c12q_l1:{ title:'Quiz: Why Wi-Fi needs more', qs:[
    { q:'Why is an attacker in a different position with Wi-Fi than with a cable?',
      opts:['just like with a cable, they must physically plug into a socket and pass building reception','it is enough to be in signal range; they can passively listen to the air from a car, without going inside','they have no advantage – a radio signal stops exactly at the outer wall of every building and never leaks out'],
      correct:1, why:'A radio signal does not stop at the wall. An attacker in range just listens passively and records the air – transmitting nothing, so you have no way to detect them.' },
    { q:'What can a shared password (WPA2-PSK) not do in a company?',
      opts:['it does not encrypt the traffic at all, so anyone in range directly reads the content of all frames','revoke access for one person, nor tell from the log who specifically was actually connected','connect more than one device at once, because only one client can use the password at a time'],
      correct:1, why:'WPA2-PSK does encrypt, but one password for all cannot be revoked for one person and the log does not tell who it was. The fix is identity per person (802.1X).' },
    { q:'What does ‘no single layer of Wi-Fi defense suffices’ mean?',
      opts:['that turning on one really strong layer (e.g. WPA3) is enough and all the others are pointless','that security is only about encryption, and authentication, isolation and detection have nothing to do with it','that encryption, authentication, PMF, isolation and detection each address a different attack and hold only together'],
      correct:2, why:'Strong encryption does not help against joining a fake AP; great authentication does not stop eavesdropping without PMF. Secure Wi-Fi = the layers together (defense in depth).' }
  ]},
  c12q_l2:{ title:'Quiz: Encryption in depth', qs:[
    { q:'What does the WPA2 four-way handshake do?',
      opts:['it sends the password over the air to the AP, which compares it with the stored one and lets the client in if they match exactly','from the PMK and random nonces it derives the session key PTK and via a MIC proves password knowledge; the password never flies','it sets up a shared public channel on the client and AP on which traffic then runs fundamentally unencrypted'],
      correct:1, why:'Password+SSID make the PMK, and from it plus nonces/MACs the session PTK that encrypts traffic and differs per connection. The password never travels; the MIC only proves knowledge of it.' },
    { q:'Why is a captured WPA2 handshake valuable to an attacker?',
      opts:['it directly contains the password in readable form, so the attacker just reads it and connects at once','it lets them try passwords offline on their machine (millions a second) – a short or dictionary one falls','it is worthless on its own, because without a permanent connection to the AP nothing can be learned from it'],
      correct:1, why:'The password is not in the handshake, but the attacker tries it offline: from a guessed password they compute a MIC and compare – without the AP, billions of tries on a GPU. The only defense: a long random password (or WPA3).' },
    { q:'How does WPA3 (SAE) improve security over WPA2-PSK?',
      opts:['each password attempt needs a live interaction with the AP (offline cracking is gone) and it adds forward secrecy','it introduces traffic encryption to Wi-Fi for the very first time, which WPA2 could not provide before','it removes the need for any password entirely, because it newly verifies clients solely by their MAC addresses'],
      correct:0, why:'SAE (Dragonfly) makes offline password guessing impossible – each attempt must go through the AP. It adds forward secrecy: previously captured traffic cannot be decrypted retroactively even if the password leaks.' },
    { q:'What does PMF (802.11w) protect?',
      opts:['the content of data frames, so even on an open passwordless network the traffic is fully encrypted','management frames – it signs them, so a forged deauth ‘the AP disconnects you’ is discarded by the client','solely the login password, which thanks to it newly does not pass through the handshake over any network at all'],
      correct:1, why:'PMF cryptographically signs management frames, so a forged deauth has no valid signature and is discarded. WPA3 enforces it, WPA2 supports it optionally – without it the deauth attack is trivial.' }
  ]},
  c12q_l3:{ title:'Quiz: 802.1X and EAP', qs:[
    { q:'What role does the AP play in 802.1X authentication?',
      opts:['it decides logins itself: it stores a database of all accounts and verifies passwords locally without a server','it is the authenticator – it only relays the login to the RADIUS server and keeps the port closed until its verdict','it plays no role; the client authenticates directly against the internet and the AP does not take part at all'],
      correct:1, why:'Three roles: supplicant (client) → authenticator (AP) → RADIUS (server with accounts). The AP does not decide, it only relays EAP and waits for the verdict; until then it passes only authentication, no data.' },
    { q:'What is the difference between EAP-TLS and PEAP/EAP-TTLS?',
      opts:['EAP-TLS has a certificate on both sides; PEAP/TTLS has one only on the server and the client sends username+password in a tunnel','EAP-TLS works only on cable, whereas PEAP and TTLS are the only methods usable on a wireless network','there is no substantial difference between them, just three trade names for an entirely identical procedure with identical certificates'],
      correct:0, why:'EAP-TLS = a certificate on both client and server (strongest, demanding on PKI). PEAP/TTLS = a certificate only on the server + username/password inside an encrypted TLS tunnel (easier to deploy).' },
    { q:'Why is verifying the server certificate critical with PEAP/TTLS?',
      opts:['it does not matter at all, because the TLS tunnel reliably protects the password even against a totally foreign server','without it the client builds a tunnel to a fake RADIUS and sends it the username and password (the PEAP attack)','only so the connection is established faster; it has no effect on the security of the login credentials'],
      correct:1, why:'The tunnel is safe only to the right server. Without verifying the certificate the client sends the username/password to the attacker’s fake AP+RADIUS. Defense: centrally enforce which CA the client trusts.' },
    { q:'What else can RADIUS do besides the ‘let in / keep out’ decision?',
      opts:['assign the client a dynamic VLAN by identity and keep accounting (who/when/how much was connected)','physically boost the AP’s radio signal so a client with a valid account always has the fastest connection','replace the firewall and switch, because after login it routes all the client’s traffic to the internet itself'],
      correct:0, why:'RADIUS returns a VLAN by role along with the verdict (one SSID, everyone to their segment) and keeps accounting – a who/when/from where/how much record, gold for incidents and SIEM.' }
  ]},
  c12q_l4:{ title:'Quiz: Guest, captive portal and NAC', qs:[
    { q:'Why is it not enough to merely ‘separate’ the guest network into its own VLAN?',
      opts:['it is entirely enough – once the guest is in its own VLAN, everything else is already fully solved automatically','the guest segment also needs its own firewall rules (default-deny, only out to the web) and ideally client isolation','on the contrary it is needless overkill – guests can be on the same VLAN as servers, nothing will happen'],
      correct:1, why:'The guest segment is treated as an untrusted zone (the course <i>Network security</i>): default-deny, only out to the web, client isolation (guests not even each other), ideally a bandwidth cap. Separating without rules is not enough.' },
    { q:'What does a captive portal do and what does it NOT do?',
      opts:['it controls access (a walled garden – before login only DNS and portal), but it does not encrypt the traffic content','it encrypts all the air traffic, so on a network with a portal no WPA or HTTPS is needed anymore','it verifies identity using a certificate on both sides, thereby also replacing both 802.1X and the RADIUS server entirely'],
      correct:0, why:'A captive portal controls only access (who may go out). It is not encryption – on an open network a neighbor still eavesdrops. And a fake portal is ideal phishing for your password.' },
    { q:'What is Enhanced Open (OWE) and what is its limit?',
      opts:['it encrypts the air even on a passwordless network; it solves eavesdropping but not a fake AP (evil twin) by itself','it requires a certificate on every client and is just another trade name for the EAP-TLS method','it is the strongest password variant, where a captured handshake cannot be broken in any way at all, not even offline'],
      correct:0, why:'OWE (Opportunistic Wireless Encryption) encrypts the air even without a password – the neighbor’s eavesdropping falls. But it does not authenticate the AP, so it does not protect against an evil twin. NAC then controls access by identity and device posture.' }
  ]},
  c12q_l5:{ title:'Quiz: Attacks on Wi-Fi', qs:[
    { q:'How does an evil twin attack work and what typically triggers it?',
      opts:['the attacker brute-forces the password directly through the AP, which luckily does not slow them after errors','a fake AP with the same SSID and a stronger signal pulls the client over (often after a deauth) and becomes MITM','it is a fault where two legitimate APs accidentally broadcast the same SSID and interfere with each other’s traffic'],
      correct:1, why:'An evil twin has the same SSID and a stronger signal; the client latches on by itself or after a forged deauth. Then the attacker sits in the middle (MITM). Defense: PMF (stops the deauth) and server verification with 802.1X.' },
    { q:'What is clever about the KARMA attack?',
      opts:['the AP answers ‘yes, that is me’ to all of a client’s probe requests, catching it even without knowing its networks','the attacker physically cuts the real AP’s power, forcing all clients to connect to the attacker’s one','it can break even a long random password, because it tries combinations directly in the AP chip’s memory at full speed'],
      correct:0, why:'A client actively shouts out the names of saved networks (probe requests). A KARMA AP answers ‘yes, that is me’ to all, so the phone connects thinking it found its network. Defense: forget old open networks.' },
    { q:'Why is WPS a weakness even with a strong WPA2 password?',
      opts:['because WPS, when enabled, automatically and forcibly changes your strong WPA2 password to the default ‘12345678’','the eight-digit PIN is verified in halves due to a design flaw → only thousands of tries, guessable in hours','it is not a weakness; WPS is on the contrary the safest way to connect and is recommended to leave enabled everywhere'],
      correct:1, why:'The WPS design flaw verifies the PIN in halves, so of 100 million combinations only a few thousand remain – the attacker guesses the PIN in hours regardless of the WPA2 password strength. So: turn WPS off.' },
    { q:'What was the KRACK attack and why is it less of a threat today?',
      opts:['an attack on the 4-way handshake by replaying message 3 (nonce reuse); it was a protocol flaw fixed by patches','an attack that guesses a password faster the longer it is; it is defended against only with very short passwords','a permanent, unfixable hole in WPA2, because of which connecting to Wi-Fi at all is discouraged today'],
      correct:0, why:'KRACK (2017) replayed handshake message 3 → the client reinstalled the key and reused the nonce, allowing part of the traffic to be decrypted. It did not depend on password strength. Vendors patched it – so: update your firmware.' }
  ]},
  c12q_l6:{ title:'Quiz: Defense and operations', qs:[
    { q:'Why are a hidden SSID and MAC filter a weak ‘defense’?',
      opts:['they are not weak – a hidden SSID and MAC filter are today a recognized full replacement for WPA encryption','both SSID and MAC fly unencrypted, so they are easily sniffed and a MAC can be cloned (security by obscurity)','they work reliably, but only in the 6 GHz band; in the older 2.4 and 5 GHz bands they cannot be enabled at all'],
      correct:1, why:'Both are security by obscurity: the SSID flies openly anyway (and the phone shouts it), the MAC is sniffed and cloned in seconds. They replace neither encryption nor authentication – real defense is WPA3, 802.1X, PMF, segmentation.' },
    { q:'What is WIPS for and what is its most valuable capability?',
      opts:['it speeds up Wi-Fi by assigning clients the best channels; it has nothing to do with security or detecting threats','sensors scan the ether and report threats; it can verify whether a foreign rogue AP hangs on your internal cabling','it is just another name for a captive portal, so it shows guests a login page with terms'],
      correct:1, why:'WIPS/WIDS are ‘cameras for the ether’: sensors scan channels and report rogue APs, evil twins, deauth, KARMA. Most valuable is detecting a rogue AP on the cabling – telling a dangerous socket-plugged AP from a harmless neighbor’s. WIPS can also act.' },
    { q:'Why are 802.1X logs key to resolving an incident?',
      opts:['thanks to accounting they give an exact who/when/from where (not ‘someone who knew the password’) and correlate in a SIEM','because on their own they can stop an attack before it even happens, thus replacing the firewall and WIPS','they are not key at all; in an incident it is best to leave the logs scattered directly in the individual boxes and devices'],
      correct:0, why:'Accounting from 802.1X/RADIUS gives an exact identity (Anna, 14:03, VLAN 10) – with a shared PSK forensics is nearly impossible. Logs are centralized off the device and correlated in a SIEM (the course <i>Advanced security (DPI, IDS/IPS)</i>) into one story.' },
    { q:'What does Zero Trust mean in relation to a Wi-Fi network?',
      opts:['that nobody is let onto the network at all and Wi-Fi is fully switched off to be safe until the workday ends','‘never trust, always verify’ even inside the network: re-verify identity and permit only the necessary (least privilege)','that behind the firewall everyone is automatically trusted, because they must have already logged in with a password once'],
      correct:1, why:'Zero Trust removes the ‘inside = trusted’ assumption an attacker relies on after a breach. Every access re-verifies identity (802.1X) and device posture (NAC), permits only the necessary and segments – even inside the network.' }
  ]},
  c12q_l7:{ title:'Quiz: The whole defense together', qs:[
    { q:'What is the main idea of layered Wi-Fi defense?',
      opts:['pick one strongest technology and rely solely on it; the other layers only slow the traffic down','each layer stops the attack a bit earlier and none suffices alone – only stacked do they hold against one trick','the order and combination of layers does not matter; the only thing that matters is having at least one security enabled'],
      correct:1, why:'Encryption stops eavesdropping, PMF and 802.1X stop evil twin/deauth, WIPS a rogue AP, segmentation and Zero Trust a breach, and logs/SIEM let you resolve the incident. No layer alone suffices – the strength is in stacking them.' },
    { q:'Where is Wi-Fi security heading (WPA3-only, DPP, 6 GHz)?',
      opts:['back to the simplicity of open passwordless networks, because encryption has proved entirely needless in practice','toward leaving transition mode (WPA3-only), connecting via QR code (DPP) and mandatory WPA3+PMF in the 6 GHz band','back to WPS and MAC filters as the main defense, complemented by hiding the SSID for more privacy'],
      correct:1, why:'The goal is WPA3-only (away with transition mode and downgrade), DPP/Wi-Fi Easy Connect instead of the leaky WPS (QR code, ideal for IoT), and 6 GHz where WPA3 and PMF are mandatory at the standard level. Security is a running process: update and leave old modes.' }
  ]}
};
const COURSE_12_FINAL_EXTRA_DEF=[{ q:'An attacker captured a WPA2 4-way handshake and drove home. Why is that enough against a weak password, and why wouldn’t WPA3 be?', opts:['the handshake can be cracked offline at millions of guesses a second; WPA3’s SAE forces online tries against the network','WPA2 sends the password in the clear right inside the handshake, whereas WPA3 at least hashes it simply','a captured handshake contains the network’s whole encryption key; WPA3 splits it across several frames'], correct:0, why:'A WPA2-PSK handshake only needs to be captured once, then passwords are tried offline – only the attacker’s hardware limits the speed. SAE (WPA3) resists offline dictionary attacks: every guess needs a fresh exchange with the network, so the rate can be throttled and a weak password bleeds far less.' },{ q:'A second AP with the same SSID and a stronger signal, no password, shows up in a café. What is this attack called, and what is the most reliable defense?', opts:['an evil twin; 802.1X with server certificate validation – the client detects a foreign network','war driving; just hide the SSID and the attacker cannot learn it, so no twin can be built','deauthentication; mainly renaming the network and a very long admin password will help'], correct:0, why:'An evil twin lures clients with a familiar network name. A password alone doesn’t help – an open twin never asks for one. With 802.1X (WPA-Enterprise) the client validates the authentication server’s certificate, so a rogue network fails that check. Hiding the SSID doesn’t stop the attack.' },{ q:'An attacker keeps kicking clients off by sending forged “disconnect” frames. What does WPA3 make mandatory against this?', opts:['PMF – protected management frames: a deauth without a valid key is discarded','a longer PSK – with a long enough password the frames can’t be forged at all','client isolation – a kicked client instantly reconnects to the network on its own'], correct:0, why:'Deauthentication frames were historically unprotected, so anyone could forge them – the basis of deauth attacks and a lead-in to an evil twin. PMF (802.11w) authenticates management frames; WPA3 requires it, while in WPA2 it is only optional.' }];
__regCourse(12,COURSE_12_DEF,COURSE_12_QUIZZES_DEF,COURSE_12_FINAL_EXTRA_DEF);
